Hi Nephi,

Please use the following prompt alongside these attached files, and see =
what CODEX comes up with.  Thanks!

=E2=80=94b
=EF=BF=BC

=EF=BF=BC=EF=BF=BC=EF=BF=BC
Statistic	Value	Domain	Variable measured	Clock starts at	=
Includes hydro routing?
Physical lag chain	3 + 3 =3D 6	Physical/mechanistic	Precip =
=E2=86=92 GSL	Watershed gauge	Yes (IS the routing)
Spectral coherence phase	7.2 =C2=B1 1.5 yr	Frequency domain	=
PDO =E2=86=92 GSL (bandpassed)	Pacific SST	Yes (full chain)
Cross-correlation peak	7 yr	Time domain	PDO =E2=86=92 GSL dV/dt	=
Pacific SST	Partial (derivative)
FFT dominant period	8 yr	Frequency domain	Resonant =
oscillation of GSL dV/dt	Not a lag (period)	N/A
Gillies Eq.1 dominant term	8 yr	Regression	PDO =E2=86=92 =
GSL elevation tendency	Pacific SST	Yes (full chain)
=EF=BF=BC=EF=BF=BC=EF=BF=BC=EF=BF=BC=EF=BF=BC=EF=BF=BC

I would like to create a v14 elevation simulator based on the gold =
standard v.10.4, with some upgrades conceptually found in attached v12.  =
But v.12 is quite buggy and I mostly don=E2=80=99t like it.   Codex, =
your job, if you choose to accept it, is to take what is good from =
somebody else=E2=80=99s code in v10.4, leave alone the tabs that are =
unaffected by the integration of the new PDO/ENSO connections and the =
policy levers, and upgrade it as described below. =20

This will require you in the planning phase to deeply analyze and =
critique each of the technical assertions embedded mathematically in the =
code, described in the text and displayed graphically.  (and check for =
self consistency) If satisfied, proceed with planning the next version. =
If concerned, itemize each inconsistency or falsehood and let=E2=80=99s =
talk.

Note that I am fond of the current styling of v10.4, its presentation =
and its graphics, especially how it compares the Tarboton stochastic =
analysis to the determinative prediction. =20

The goal is that we want to create a simulator that is largely the same =
as the vetted v10.4, but that accounts for the new mathematical =
modifications as described in the attached papers.  We=E2=80=99ll be =
more confident if it is successful if we see a lake elevation bump in =
2029-2030.  Note that the attached v.12 failed in this regard.

A couple of other changes we are planning in this version, that were at =
least partially successful in the attached v12 (please review all =
materials, come back with a plan and any clarifying questions):

(1) create a simplified first tab without a lot of technical clutter, =
but that has just two levers: conservation and augmentation. This will =
be used for education purposes.  It should span the same time period as =
the main simulator tab, and show the same uncertainty envelope around =
the forecast.
2) Then the current simulator tab can move to the second tab position =
with the following changes: Move the Combined model comparison with its =
blue and red explanation boxes to the top of the cross-validation tab.=20=

3) Place the strike team policy levers below the graph on the simulator =
tab and for each lever we need to enable a year-by-year accounting =
methodology, because there is no way any particular lever can deliver a =
consistent amount of water as the source constantly changes. e.g., ag =
optimization and leasing will only work following good water years. Like =
this year, the streams are dry but reservoirs still have banked water. =
So water rights may be diverted from storage systems to GSL. But if the =
next year is also dry, that strategy is screwed. Likewise, Newfoundland =
has water this year, but if we bring it back to the lake, then that is a =
one-time benefit. Mineral extraction at USMag has ceased. so we are not =
drawing that water out. But accounting wise, I guess we get to count it =
every year. Similarly phragmites...=20


This turn,=20
(1) Please read and modify the RC white paper embedded in v.10.4 and =
update according to the addendum and documents attached here.  This new =
document will be needed to support the v14 upcoming, in a similar =
embedded fashion.   Compare your version to the one embedded in v12, and =
let=E2=80=99s discuss any major differences.

(2) create a plan for implementing the new v14 simulator

=46rom v. 10.4 Please keep the  following tabs intact: Damped =
Oscillator, how it works, USGS bathymetry, Glossary, Data explorer=20

=46rom V12, keep the =E2=80=9Cwater budget=E2=80=9D tab, the model and =
methods, and the version history tab, noting that v.10 was in March, =
2026. v12 and v13 were unsuccessful attempts to implement the above =
changes.  On v.14, please add an appropriate citation for the efforts by =
Codex, augmenting what has previously been done in Perplexity.

(3) please note that the RC white paper should just have my authorship =
without reference to anyone else yet. I still need to separately invite =
their participation.=20

Attached find some graphs that belong embedded in the white paper, which =
in turn, should be embedded in the clickable HTML elevation simulator =
app.  If, in your questioning and planning phase, you find they are =
better suited embedded into a different tab, I will certainly entertain =
your suggestion.  I=E2=80=99ll also entertain your recommendation on =
re-ordering the tabs for better logical flow.

Below find some random notes from the failed v12-13 threads that may or =
may not be helpful as you plan your approach.  They should be used in =
guiding, not determining your direction and outcomes, But they may be =
helpful in framing questions that I can answer in the first turn.

On the Overview tab, I change the sliders and nothing changes on the =
graph.  does it work?  I would also like the graph to begin at 1977 for =
context.  The intent was that this should be a simplified version of the =
main simulator  On the simulator tab I like the graphical presentation =
of v10.4, particularly becasue of the uncertainty cone surrounding the =
projection.  On V12, again, I don=E2=80=99t see that any of the policy =
levers actually have an effect on the projection, either from the slider =
or from the annual accounting tabs.  Please verify that these are =
working, and if they are, please implement the v12 annual accounting =
(and the new PDO-ENSO correlation) into the v10.4 visualization. On the =
Cross-validation tab, this tab name applies to the V10.4, which we will =
keep.  The v12 cross validation tab will be retained in v14 but renamed =
as =E2=80=9CTesting Hypotheses=E2=80=9D.  NOTE here that the table in =
this tab indicates 61% skill for forecasting in the five year horizon.  =
We want to forecast out to the 2036 horizon and let the uncertainty cone =
expand accordingly.=20


Some clarifications: the purpose of the Overview tab is to provide a =
very simple entry point, useful for educational purposes and =
implementing the RC enabled forecasting but without the other =
distractions. Q2: yes, 2034 is a milestone marker. The 2026 PDO/ENSO =
effects are expected to be lagged to 2030-31, correct? yes, note the =
skill % drop at 2036. Q3: So the annual accounting functions only apply =
in the "simulator" tab. Please verify that any changes to any of the =
years would be reflected both in downstream effects from the same lever, =
as well as manifest in the total trend (yes, "drive" per you 3(c) =
question). I expect that one-time changes (reservoir release etc) will =
not make a huge difference, but phragmites and mineral changes will =
reflect every year. Q4: I think the PDO/ENSO should be fixed according =
to our best understanding of the current state (or the combined ENSO now =
with the state of PDO 3+3 years ago. We already look at the weather, so =
we should do a real-time check of the PDO /ENSO. Now, what may be =
valuable would be to change these in the timeframe beyond the known =
(spring 2027 for ENSO, post 3+3 for PDO?) that would enable some =
scenario based forecasting beyond the known window. Can you come up with =
a way to do that? Q5: Are you saying that v10.4 thought the then-current =
elevation was 4192? If so, then yes, definitely update this. Q6: on =
versioning: yes v10 in March. Skip v.12 documentation and incorporate =
the PDO/ENSO integration into V13.=

